From 217b590b0545a5b6f917c8b15c1d9aea7c127ca4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon Williams <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:12:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Bluetooth: SMP: ignore an all-zero IRK

The Security Manager specification defines an all-zero Identity
Resolving Key as "the device does not have a valid resolvable private
address" (Core Specification, Vol 3, Part H, 3.6.4). Such a key cannot
resolve anything the peer sends.

smp_cmd_ident_addr_info() stores it anyway. Every resolvable private
address seen afterwards is then checked against the zero key, and about
one in 2^24 of them matches by chance, making an unrelated device look
like the bonded peer.

Do not store an all-zero IRK. Key distribution continues as before, and
the peer's other keys are kept under its connection address.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Brandon Williams <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
---
 net/bluetooth/smp.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/net/bluetooth/smp.c b/net/bluetooth/smp.c
index b0b0d25e3f86..513ad237611f 100644
--- a/net/bluetooth/smp.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/smp.c
@@ -2678,6 +2678,15 @@ static int smp_cmd_ident_addr_info(struct l2cap_conn *conn,
 		goto distribute;
 	}
 
+	/* An all-zero IRK means the peer has no resolvable private address
+	 * (Core Specification, Vol 3, Part H, 3.6.4). Storing it would only
+	 * let unrelated resolvable private addresses match it by chance.
+	 */
+	if (!memchr_inv(smp->irk, 0, sizeof(smp->irk))) {
+		bt_dev_dbg(hcon->hdev, "ignoring all-zero IRK");
+		goto distribute;
+	}
+
 	bacpy(&smp->id_addr, &info->bdaddr);
 	smp->id_addr_type = info->addr_type;
 
